## HLA expression in edited cells
Our editing strategy produces HLA-E single-chain genes that are expressed from the \( B2M \) promoter, so they should be regulated appropriately in response to cytokines. Undifferentiated ESCs with the \( B2M \) genotypes shown in Figure 1 were cultured with and without IFN- \( \gamma \), and the levels of cell surface HLA class I molecules were determined by flow cytometry (Fig. 2). In the absence of IFN- \( \gamma \), \( B2M^{+/+} \) ESCs expressed nearly undetectable amounts of both HLA-E and polymorphic class I molecules (HLA-A, B and C), all of which were upregulated significantly in the presence of IFN- \( \gamma \). In contrast, \( B2M^{-/-} \) ESCs had no surface expression of HLA class I molecules, even in the presence of IFN- \( \gamma \), confirming that these cells have a complete knockout of B2M (although they acquired an abnormal tetraploid karyotype).
## HLA-E single-chain expression prevents NK-mediated lysis
Undifferentiated ESCs have limited immunogenicity and do not induce NK-mediated lysis24, so we differentiated ESCs into \( \mathrm{CD45^{+}} \) hematopoietic derivatives (Fig. 3a) before characterizing their immune reactivity. Flow cytometry showed there were \( >89\% \) \( \mathrm{CD45^{+}} \) hematopoietic cells on day 38 in all edited cell lines except
class-I-negative controls, since \(B2M^{-/-}\) \(\mathrm{CD45^{+}}\) cells could not be produced in adequate numbers.
